   "source": [
    "## Part 2: Explanation & Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "| Circuit | Gates Applied | Theoretical State | Dominant Outcomes | Observed Results (100 shots) | Correlation Type |\n",
    "| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n",
    "| **Original** | $H_0, \\; CNOT_{0,1}$ | $\\vert{}\\Phi^+\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\vert{}00\\rangle + \\vert{}11\\rangle)$ | `00`, `11` | e.g., `{'00': 52, '11': 48}` | Correlated (Both qubits always yield the same bit) |\n",
    "| **Modified** | $H_0, \\; CNOT_{0,1}, \\; X_1$ | $\\vert{}\\Psi^+\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\vert{}01\\rangle + \\vert{}10\\rangle)$ | `01`, `10` | e.g., `{'01': 49, '10': 51}` | Anti-correlated (The qubits always yield opposite bits) |\n",
    "\n",
    "### How the Modification Changed the Outcomes\n",
    "1. **Correlation Inversion:** In the original circuit, the $CNOT$ entangles the target qubit $q_1$ with $q_0$, causing both qubits to yield identical values upon measurement (either both `0` or both `1`)[cite: 3].\n",
    "2. **Impact of the $X$ Gate:** Adding an $X$ (NOT) gate to $q_1$ applies a deterministic bit-flip after entanglement. Whenever the state would have collapsed to $\\vert{}00\\rangle$, the second qubit is flipped to $\\vert{}01\\rangle$. Likewise, $\\vert{}11\\rangle$ is converted to $\\vert{}10\\rangle$.\n",
    "3. **Outcome Transition:** The modification inverts the correlation without destroying entanglement: the qubits remain maximally entangled, but the joint measurement outcomes transition entirely from symmetric pairs (`00`, `11`) to complementary pairs (`01`, `10`)[cite: 3]."
   ]
